 "name": "Small Urban Parks: Perceived Restorativeness of Four Park Types",
 "description": "Method replication with the paper's own photographs: participants view the 24 stimulus photographs of small urban parks in Fuzhou, six for each of four park types (pocket parks, linear riverside parks, community parks, small comprehensive parks). The types come in random order; within each type the six photos appear in random order for 10 s each with a 5 s pause, as in the paper. After each type, participants rate it on the Perceived Restorativeness Scale (being away, fascination, coherence, compatibility). Rebuilt from Weng, Chen, Lin, Chi & Li (2025), Frontiers in Public Health 13: 1667502, with the stimuli printed in its Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0). Analysis as in the paper: mean of each PRS dimension per park type; compare park types with gender as a covariate (MANCOVA, Bonferroni post-hoc). Differences from the original study: (1) the paper does not print its PRS items or scale length; the English 16-item wording comes from Frontiers in Public Health 10:801453 (Table 5) and the 1-7 range is our choice; (2) no eye tracking, skin conductance or heart-rate variability; (3) no backward digit-span stress induction and no 5-min washout between park types; (4) photos are cropped from the published figure (about 420 x 230 px); (5) PRS is asked once after each park type rather than before and after viewing.",
